@@ -69,6 +69,24 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON. The output is valid JSON, re-serialized over multiple lines with an indent of 4 spaces by default (set the ``indent`` argument to change this). Syntax highlighting is applied unless you pass ``highlight=False``::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+JSON renderables can also be printed or logged directly, with ``console.print(JSON(...))`` or ``console.log(JSON(...))``. The renderable disables word wrapping, so by default long lines are cropped to the console width. Pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to keep each line whole.
+
+The :func:`rich.print_json` function is also available at package level, and uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line by running the ``rich.json`` module with the path to a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
